## Schema File Preview (SF)

A schema, read as a list of fields. Consumes `SchemaFieldSummaryField`.

```gts
<SchemaFilePreview
  @fields={{@model.schemaFields}}
  @rootType={{@model.rootType}}
  @searchable={{true}}
  @onSelectField={{this.inspect}}
/>
```

### What it leads with, and why

The reader opened a data file and needs to know what is in it. So the table leads with **path, type, and whether it is required** — the three facts that answer *"can I rely on this key"* — and demotes the description to supporting text.

### Nesting is indent, not a tree

A collapsible tree makes the reader click to find out whether a field **exists**. Indentation derived from `depth` shows the whole shape at rest, which is what a preview is for. Deep schemas scroll; they do not fold. Indent caps at four levels — past that it stops buying clarity and starts eating the column.

### Fill rate is drawn, not printed

A percentage in a column is a number the eye parses one row at a time. A bar makes *"this key is usually missing"* visible while scanning, with the figure beside it for anyone who needs the exact number.

A field with **no sampling** renders an em-dash, not an empty bar. An unmeasured field and a never-present one must not look the same — that is the same rule `fillRate` enforces by returning undefined rather than 0.

### Required is weight, not a column

A whole column spent on a boolean is a column not spent on the schema. Required rows carry it in the field name's weight.

### Accessibility and responsiveness

The field name is a real `<button>` — the row is not clickable, so keyboard and focus work without inventing roles. The table is the one element allowed to exceed the container, inside its own `overflow-x` wrapper, so the page never scrolls sideways. Below 420px the bar and the description drop; the figure stays, because the bar was the decoration and the number was the fact.

### Non-goals

Does not read or parse a file. Does not validate. Not an editor.
